Having only one MySQL server capable of accepting writes in a production environment can lead to several potential issues related to SPOF, scalability, availability, performance, and fault tolerance. 

Having servers with all it's components can lead to the following:
1. Scalability Issues
Resource Contention: When a server handles multiple roles (database, web server, and application server), these components can compete for the same resources (CPU, memory, disk I/O). High resource usage by one component (e.g., database queries) can negatively impact the performance of other components (e.g., web server response times).
Limited Scalability: Scaling out a monolithic server means duplicating all components, which is inefficient. For example, if only the web server needs more capacity, you still have to scale the database and application server, leading to unnecessary resource usage and costs.
2. Maintenance Complexity
Downtime for Updates: Updates or maintenance to one component require taking the entire server offline, affecting all services hosted on it. This leads to higher downtime and impacts the availability of the entire application.
Complex Upgrades: Upgrading one part of the stack (e.g., database) might introduce compatibility issues with other parts (e.g., web server or application server), complicating the maintenance process.
3. Reliability and Fault Tolerance
Single Point of Failure: If a server fails, all components (database, web server, and application server) go down simultaneously, leading to complete service disruption. This setup lacks redundancy and increases the risk of extended downtime.
Isolation of Failures: Issues in one component (e.g., a bug in the application server) can cascade and affect the performance or stability of other components on the same server.
4. Resource Management
Inefficient Resource Utilization: Different components have varying resource needs. For example, databases may need more disk I/O and memory, while web servers might need more CPU and network bandwidth. A monolithic server setup cannot optimize resources for the specific needs of each component, leading to inefficient resource utilization.
Difficulty in Monitoring and Tuning: It is harder to monitor, diagnose, and tune performance issues when multiple critical components share the same resources. Isolating the root cause of performance degradation can be more challenging.
5. Deployment and Development Flexibility
Slower Deployments: Deploying updates to one component requires redeploying the entire monolithic server, which can slow down the deployment process and increase the risk of introducing errors.
Lack of Modular Development: Development teams might face difficulties in adopting agile practices when all components are tightly coupled, reducing flexibility and slowing down innovation.

